message: "If you use this preprint, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "The Constitution as a Founding Inscription: What Kind of Social Object a National Constitution Is, and How It Institutionalizes the Human Condition Across the World's Legal Traditions"
authors:
  - family-names: Bourguet
    given-names: Luis
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4673-4486"
    affiliation: "LabOnt — Centre for Ontology, Università di Torino; CSAL — Centro de Estudios Latinoamericanos, Università di Bologna"
abstract: >
  This article brings together two questions usually kept apart: the
  anthropological-philosophical question of the human condition and the
  ontological-legal question of what kind of social object a national
  constitution is. The thesis merges both: a constitution is a founding
  inscription — a sovereign, recursive, generative and maximally unamendable
  social object — operating as the mechanism through which a political
  community stabilizes its answers to the human condition. The framework
  integrates Ferraris's New Realism, Bunge's and Romero's systemic
  materialism, Glenn's theory of legal traditions as transmitted normative
  information, Todorov's theory of alterity, and Anderson's and De Botton's
  functional reading of religion. The thesis is tested against constitutional
  orders from every continent and every major legal tradition — civil, common,
  chthonic, Talmudic, Islamic, Hindu, East Asian, socialist and African —
  drawing on the Comparative Constitutions Project.
keywords:
  - social object
  - founding inscription
  - legal traditions
  - unamendability
  - comparative constitutionalism
  - documentality
  - New Realism
  - systemic materialism
  - alterity
  - human condition
